  "notes": "J Records teen-to-adult soloist built on Scott Storch's 80s-synth R&B plus a Ne-Yo topline; Storch has his own model, so this is the artist lane, 2002–2009. The research gives \"65–98 straight; ballads 61–66 half-time\", and the straight band is the base with the mode at 94, the sourced tempo of the signature record. Three drum deltas quote the entry's phrase for the kit — \"mechanized melodic backbeat with a sturdy, clap-heavy drum track\": `quantizeStrength` is 0.92 with `offGridMs` 0 and `swing.amount` 0.52 (mechanized, against this genre's under-quantised 0.62), the clap is a real stack with a velocity-tier accent band of 110–124 over a 88–102 main (clap-heavy), and \"kick on 1 and the 16th before 3\" puts the secondary anchor on `2a`. Harmony is the sourced progression: \"`iv–i–VII` in G minor (Cm–Gm–F)… a modal three-chord loop with 'wandering 80s-style synth pillows'\", so `iv–i–VII` is the top family with by far the largest weight, `maxChords` is 4 (a cap, not a target — it is only there so the one pop-crossover family is not truncated) and the counter is a `sustain_pad`-led pillow at +12. \"Light tenor with Ne-Yo-written narrow-interval phrasing\" is a 0.86 step share and a near-zero octave jump. The low end is the entry's explicit correction of the lane default — \"'soulful bass' playing a moving line rather than a static sub, doubled by keys; Storch's low end is keyboard-derived, not 808-derived\" — so there is no `drums.bass808` block, `followRootsProb` falls to 0.56 and the cells move. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, all density numbers and the swing figure.",
